Quick Book Revision

To delete the kth node of a doubly linked list, first handle the empty list and K == 1 (head) cases. Otherwise, traverse to the kth node. Connect its previous node to its next node using current.prev.next = current.next, and connect its next node back using current.next.prev = current.prev. Finally, disconnect the deleted node by setting its prev and next to null, then return head.

Time: O(k) — traverse up to the kth node; worst case O(n).
Auxiliary Space: O(1).



/*
Definition for a Node.

class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;

    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthElement(ListNode head, int K) {

        // Case 1: Empty list
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Case 2: K = 1 means delete the head
        if (K == 1) {
            head = head.next;

            // If a new head exists, remove its backward connection
            if (head != null) {
                head.prev = null;
            }

            return head;
        }

        // Start traversal from the head
        ListNode current = head;

        // Move current to the Kth node
        for (int i = 1; i < K; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        // current is now the Kth node

        // Connect the previous node to the next node
        if (current.prev != null) {
            current.prev.next = current.next;
        }

        // Connect the next node back to the previous node
        if (current.next != null) {
            current.next.prev = current.prev;
        }

        // Completely disconnect the deleted node
        current.prev = null;
        current.next = null;

        // Return the head of the modified list
        return head;
    }
}
